                                                                    Windows Update - Related Failures

          ## Issue ##  #
- Windows Update fails to download or install updates.
- Error messages such as Update failed, Something went wrong, or specific error codes appear.
- Updates become stuck at a percentage (example: 0%, 20%, 99%).
- The system repeatedly asks to restart but updates never complete.

      ## Cause ## (Root Cause / Most Likely Causes)
- Insufficient disk space.
- Corrupted Windows Update cache or components.
- Network or DNS issues preventing update downloads.
- Corrupted system files.
- Windows Update services not running correctly.


# Steps / Resolution #

 1. Restart the Computer
- Restarting clears temporary update locks and pending operations.

 2. Check Internet Connectivity
- Ensure the device has a stable internet connection.
- Windows updates require reliable access to Microsoft update servers.

3. Run Windows Update Troubleshooter
Navigate to: Settings - Update & Security - Troubleshoot - Windows Update

Select **Run the troubleshooter**.

![Windows Update Troubleshooter](screenshots/screenshot1.png)

---

## 4. Restart Windows Update Services

Press **Win + R** - type: services.msc


Restart the following services:

### Windows Update
- Controls detection, download, and installation of Windows updates.

### Background Intelligent Transfer Service (BITS)
- Handles background downloading of update files.

# Cryptographic Services
- Verifies update file authenticity and integrity.

![Restart Services](screenshots/screenshot2.png)

# 5. Clear the Windows Update Cache
1. Stop the **Windows Update service** in "services.msc".
2. Navigate to: C:\Windows\SoftwareDistribution
3. Delete all contents inside this folder.
4. Restart the **Windows Update service**.

![Clear Update Cache](screenshots/screenshot3.png)

Clearing the Windows Update cache removes corrupted or incomplete temporary update files and forces Windows to download fresh update data.


## 6. Run System File Repairs
Open **Command Prompt as Administrator** and run:
 sfc /scannow

If issues persist, run: DISM /Online /Cleanup-Image /RestoreHealth
 this command scans windows system files for courrption 


## 7 Check disk space
Note: Checking disk space is often one of the first things to verify before installing updates.


# 8 Manually install updates
If updates continue to fail, install them manually.

How to manually install a Windows update
Identify the failed update
Open Settings: - Update & Security - Windows Update - View update history
Note the KB number of the failed update (example: KB5006670)

Go to Microsoft Update Catalog
Open your browser:
https://www.catalog.update.microsoft.com

Search for the KB number
Download the correct update
Choose the version matching your system:

Windows 10 / Windows 11
32-bit (x86) or 64-bit (x64)
Install the update manually: Double-click the downloaded .msu file
This launches the Windows Update Standalone Installer

Follow the prompts to install the update
Restart the computer
Some updates require a restart to fully apply.
Verify installation

Go to Settings - Update & Security - View update history
Confirm the update shows as Successfully Installed

Manually installing updates bypasses issues such as corrupted update cache, service failures, or network download problems.



          ## Why It Happened ##
Windows Update failures may  occur when update components become corrupted, requiring services stop running, or the system cannot properly download update files.
Restarting services restores update functionality, clearing the update cache removes corrupted temporary files, and running SFC and DISM repairs essential Windows system components.
Ensuring adequate disk space allows updates to install properly, while manually installing updates bypasses issues affecting the automatic update process.
